WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.500
School choice advocates received a pretty strong precedent with today's ruling.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.780
On June 30th the U.S. supreme court delivered a major win for school choice and religious liberty

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:13.320
in a case argued by the libertarian public interest law firm, the Institute for Justice.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.580
Writing for the 5-4 majority Chief Justice John Roberts declared that a state need not

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.080
subsidize private education.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:24.740
But once a state decides to do so, it cannot disqualify some private schools solely because they are religious.

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:29.820
The plaintiff in the case, Kendra Espinoza, was able to send her kids to a private religious school in Montana

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.960
thanks to a state scholarship subsidy program which supplemented the extra income she made

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.560
as a bookkeeper and night janitor.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:39.760
In 2018 the Montana Supreme Court killed that program on the grounds that it violated

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.440
the state constitutional prohibition on public funding for religious schools.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.540
Justice Roberts found that the Montana ruling expressly discriminates on the basis of religious status.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.540
That decision strengthens an earlier high court ruling that upheld the constitutionality

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:55.120
of scholarship programs for sectarian schools.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.400
According to the Institute for Justice the decision will have an impact on most

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:03.940
of the 14 states where scholarship students who want to attend religious schools still face legal obstacles.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:09.080
